{"groups":[{"members":["c1","c2"],"primary":"c1","title":"San Diego Residential Tenant Protections (Just Cause for Termination of Tenancy)","requirement":"A landlord may not terminate a covered residential tenancy without at-fault just cause (such as non-payment, material lease breach, nuisance or criminal activity) or good-faith no-fault just cause (owner or family move-in, withdrawal from the rental market, compliance with a government or court vacate order, or demolition or substantial remodel), and the termination notice must state the cause. No-fault terminations require 30 or 60 days' notice under Civ. Code § 1946.1, relocation assistance, notice to the San Diego Housing Commission, and in specified cases a right of first offer to re-rent within five years; buyout agreements are regulated and retaliation is prohibited.","key_value":"Relocation assistance for no-fault termination: 2 months' actual rent, or 3 months' actual rent if the tenant is a senior (62+) or disabled. It is paid regardless of tenant income or length of tenancy, either directly (due within 15 days of the notice) or as an equal rent waiver, at the landlord's option.","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2023-06-24","end_date":null,"citation":"San Diego Municipal Code §§ 98.0701–98.0710 (ch. 9, art. 8, div. 7; Ord. O-21647 N.S.)","coverage_conditions":"Applies to residential rental property in the City of San Diego (any dwelling or unit intended for human habitation, including rented dwellings in a mobilehome park) with a tenancy of more than 30 days, subject to the listed exemptions.","exemptions":"Transient and tourist hotel occupancy; short-term occupancy of 30 consecutive days or less; deed-restricted or regulatory-restricted affordable housing and housing under an affordable housing subsidy agreement (Section 8 Housing Choice Voucher tenancies are not exempt); mobilehomes subject to the Mobilehome Residency Law; nonprofit hospital, religious facility, extended care facility, licensed residential care facility for the elderly, adult residential facility, or nonprofit transitional housing; dormitories of educational institutions; units where the tenant shares a bathroom or kitchen with a landlord who lives on site; housing with a certificate of occupancy issued within the previous 15 years; certain owner-occupied single-family and duplex properties.","coverage_from":"c2","conflict_note":null}],"dropped":[]}
